{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Restart done and verified. **Reclaimed ~3.75 GB of RAM. And the before/after gives the number that matters: this is growth, not a working set.**\n\n**Result:**\n\n    BEFORE   Mem: total 7813  used 6865  free 186   available 680\n             rebar-mcp-59d504c2bdbb-8093   4.566GiB / 7.631GiB   59.84%\n\n    AFTER    Mem: total 7813  used 3108  free 3399  available 4501\n             rebar-mcp-59d504c2bdbb-8093   368.5MiB / 7.631GiB    4.72%\n\nHost memory went from 88% used with 680 MB available to 40% used with **4,501 MB available**.\n\n**Verified recovered, not merely restarted:**\n\n    docker ps:  rebar-mcp-59d504c2bdbb-8093 | Up About a minute (healthy)\n    curl localhost:8093/     -> 404      (responding; root is not a page)\n    curl localhost/mcp/      -> 301      (nginx routing to a live upstream)\n    curl localhost:8080/     -> 200      (gerrit)\n    curl https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/ -> 200\n\nThe 404 and 301 are the point: they are ANSWERS. This ticket's failure mode is a 502 from a dead upstream, and there is none. I used `docker restart` rather than remove-and-recreate specifically so the container kept its name and nginx's upstream include stayed valid — this ticket records that removing an mcp container nginx still points at is what turned a transient exit into a permanent 502 on 2026-09-02.\n\n**The growth rate, which is the actual finding.** A freshly started MCP is **368.5 MiB**. The container it replaced had been up **2 hours** and reached **4.566 GiB**. That is roughly **+4.2 GB in two hours** on a 7.6 GiB host.\n\nThat resolves the open question from my previous comment. I said someone should establish whether 4.57 GB is a leak or a legitimate working set for a ~254k-commit store. It is not a working set: a working set does not start at 368 MB and reach 4.57 GB in two hours while the store barely changes. This is unbounded growth, and at that rate the host is back to OOM territory in roughly two to three hours.\n\n**So the restart is a reprieve, not a fix**, and I want that stated plainly so nobody reads \"reclaimed 3.75 GB\" as resolution. On this trajectory the next OOM is this afternoon. The real fix is whatever bounds that allocation — and the fact that `mcp_mem_abort` and `mcp_retire_cap` metrics already exist suggests someone anticipated MCP memory needing management, so the mechanism may exist and be misconfigured rather than absent.\n\n**One diagnostic that is now possible and was not before.** `container_memory_rss_bytes{service=mcp}` returned NO DATA for the last ten hours because memory pressure was starving the later sections of `observability.sh`. With pressure relieved, that metric should start publishing again — which means the growth curve can be watched directly instead of reconstructed from `docker stats` snapshots. If it stays blind, that is its own defect worth chasing.\n\nNot closing anything. The 502 mechanism is now evidenced end to end: unbounded MCP growth -> host memory exhaustion -> `git` (invoked constantly by gates and the review-bot) triggers the OOM killer -> kernel kills the largest RSS, which is the MCP -> endpoint 502s. The 03:13 UTC kill recorded in my previous comment is that chain completing once already today."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788611799691987500,"uuid":"50bd7bb1-2a87-494c-ade8-4a040cf41596"}